Features of Java
1) Simple
Java is simple because:
Pointers are not directly used like in C/C++.
Memory management is done by JVM rather than the programmer.
Helps avoid memory leaks.

Memory Areas
Stack Frame → Limited memory.
Heap → Borrowed from OS.
In C/C++
malloc() → allocates memory in heap.
free() → returns memory back to heap.

In Java new keyword allocates memory in heap.
No need for delete.

2) Object Oriented
a) Abstraction
Hiding implementation details of a process.
User can still use it.

b) Encapsulation
Used to keep data together.
Protects data from the outside world.
Basis of Encapsulation
1. Class Definition
2. Access Specifiers
Public
Private
Protected
Default

3. Scope
Scope Rule
Outer scope cannot access inner scope directly.
Inner scope can access outer scope.
Example:
“Seeing people from a building, but they cannot see you.”

c) Inheritance
One class inherits features of another class.
Helps reduce repeated code.
Subclass can use features of superclass.

d) Polymorphism
One name with different meanings.

Example:
Same function name works differently for different data types.

Example in Java:
Math.abs(int)
Math.abs(long)
Math.fabs(float)

3) Robust (Reliable)
In C/C++
int no;
printf("%d", no);
Prints garbage value.
In Java
Gives error if variable is not initialized.
Integer values are not defaulted randomly.

4) Distributed
One server can host thousands of websites.
Clients can access from all over the world.
If one server is busy, request can be redirected to another server.
Networking
Uses:

Networking protocols

java.net

5) Secured

JRE ensures applications do not directly access hardware or OS.

Protects against viruses and unsafe operations.

6) Multithreading

Multiple tasks can run simultaneously.
7) Portable

“Write Once, Run Anywhere.”

