Problems in Above Design

This code works but is considered poor design because:

1. Main method becomes complicated.


2. OOP principle says:

Attributes/Data members should be private.

Methods should be public.



3. Repeated method calls increase memory usage.



Rectangle Class Example

Rectangle Class

class Rectangle
{
    private int length, breadth;
}

Why Private?

Variables cannot be accessed directly from main().



---

Setter and Getter Methods

Setter Method

Used to initialize object fields.

Takes arguments.


public void setDim(int l, int b)
{
    length = l;
    breadth = b;
}


Getter Methods

Non-void methods.

Return values.

Usually do not take arguments.

Operate on object data.


Area Method

A = l \times b

public int getArea()
{
    return length * breadth;
}


Perimeter Method

P = 2(l+b)

public int getPerimeter()
{
    return 2 * (length + breadth);
}

Executable Class for Rectangle

Notes

One Java file can contain multiple classes.

Only one class can be public.

Java file name should be same as public class name.



public class prog2
{
    public static void main(String args[])
    {
        Rectangle r1 = new Rectangle();

        // r1.length = 20;   // Error because variable is private

        r1.setDim(20, 10);

        System.out.println(
            "Area of rectangle r1 is "
            + r1.getArea()
        );

        System.out.println(
            "Perimeter of rectangle r1 is "
            + r1.getPerimeter()
        );
    }
}



